          "evidence_text": "Frozen duration regression trained on 1611046 January-March 2025 eligible US flights; April has 577730 and May has 597574 evaluation flights.\nApril 2025: model RMSE 49.732398 minutes versus training-mean baseline 49.966953; model MAE 21.083687 minutes versus training-median baseline 14.563043.\nMay 2025: model RMSE 55.669319 minutes versus training-mean baseline 56.171814; model MAE 23.191613 minutes versus training-median baseline 18.162460.\nAmong 52142 observed May delays of at least 60 minutes, model MAE was 124.546406 minutes and bias (predicted minus actual) was -124.543778 minutes.\nThe duration model failed the preset 1% RMSE improvement check in both April and May; duration inference is not deployed. This already inspected US release is not a fresh holdout, causal explanation or operational approval.\nTarget is max(0, arrival-delay minutes), without upper clipping, conditional on completed non-diverted flights with observed arrivals.\nThe artifact and April evaluation were frozen before the May query; earlier project experiments had already inspected this five-month release.\nSquared-error fitting targets conditional means; improvement in RMSE can coexist with worse MAE than a median baseline.\nThe severe-delay group is defined retrospectively using actual outcomes, not identified prospectively by this model.\nThese measurements do not identify why the model underperformed. No weather evidence, refitting, threshold change, uncertainty interval or individual-flight estimate is supplied."
